  "slug": "dismutase-history-horizon-d1a8",
  "summary": "New apple, pear and Arabidopsis studies pin redox outputs on specific kinases, E3 ligases, transcription factors and splicing regulators.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is redox chemistry acting through defined regulatory modules. In apple, MxMPK4-1 phosphorylates NADP-malic enzyme MxME3-7 at S201, enhancing ROS signaling and iron-deficiency tolerance via malate metabolism. In pear, PUB8 targets ZAT11/12 for degradation, while ZAT11/12 activate LAC1 to drive ROS-linked lignin polymerization and stone cell formation. Two Arabidopsis preprints extend this logic. H2S persulfidation and PRMT5-dependent LSm4 methylation connect H2S signaling to alternative splicing during stomatal closure. Peroxisomal H2O2 homeostasis under PEG-induced water stress rebalances ABA and GA metabolism, slowing germination. Ascorbate control by MdMYB70/MdbHLH62 via MdGGP2 and MdAPX1 in ursolic acid-treated fresh-cut apple is closely related. Less redox-centered are miR159a–BrMYB12 control of myo-inositol/raffinose biosynthesis and thermotolerance, melatonin plus selenium nanoparticles acting via MAPK signaling in salt-stressed soybean, and m6A–metabolome mapping of drought responses in wheat.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
